    ('pte-em-rwfib-148', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'reading', 'fill-in-blanks-rw', 'Fill in the Blanks (Dropdown)',
     'From The Earliest Civilisations', 'Below is a text with blanks. Click on each blank, a list of choices will appear. Select the appropriate answer choice for each blank.',
     'From the earliest civilisations, plants and animals have been portrayed as a means of understanding and recording the potential uses, such as their economic and healing properties. From the first illustrated [[b1]] of medicinal plants, De Materia Medica by Dioscorides, in the first century through to the late fourteenth century, the illustration of plants and animals changed very little. Woodcuts in instructional manuals and herbals were often repeatedly copied over the centuries, resulting in a loss of definition and accuracy so that they became little more than stylized decoration. With the growing [[b2]] of copperplate engravings, the traditional use of woodcuts declined and the representation of plants and animals became more accurate. Then, with the [[b3]] of artists such as Albrecht Durer and Leonardo Da Vinci, naturalists such as Otto Brunfels, Leonhard Fuchs in botany and Conrad Gesner and Ulisse Aldrovandi in zoology, nature began to be depicted in a more realistic style. Individual living plants or animals were [[b4]] directly and their likeness rendered onto paper or vellum.', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '[{"id": "b1", "options": ["separate", "deletion", "catalogue", "segregate"], "correctAnswer": "catalogue"}, {"id": "b2", "options": ["understanding", "popularity", "options", "unusualness"], "correctAnswer": "popularity"}, {"id": "b3", "options": ["deactivation", "outgrowth", "emergence", "abandon"], "correctAnswer": "emergence"}, {"id": "b4", "options": ["observed", "overlooked", "ignorant"], "correctAnswer": "observed"}]'::jsonb,
