     'One of the most [[b1]] of psychologists, Clark Hull, claimed that the essence of reasoning lies in the putting together of two ''behavior segments'' in some [[b2]] way, never actually performed before, so as to reach a [[b3]]. Two followers of Clark Hull, Howard and Tracey Kendler, devised a test for children that was [[b4]] based on Clark Hull''s principles. The children were given the [[b5]] of learning to operate a machine so as to get a toy. In order to succeed they had to go through a two-stage sequence.', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '[{"id": "b1", "options": ["playful", "eminent", "clear", "conspicuous"], "correctAnswer": "eminent"}, {"id": "b2", "options": ["novel", "unexplained", "bougous", "vague"], "correctAnswer": "novel"}, {"id": "b3", "options": ["place", "point", "goal", "might"], "correctAnswer": "goal"}, {"id": "b4", "options": ["vivdly", "explicitly", "probably", "only"], "correctAnswer": "explicitly"}, {"id": "b5", "options": ["chance", "time", "fervor", "task"], "correctAnswer": "task"}]'::jsonb,
